papers.abstract:
We prove the reverse logarithmic Kodaira inequality for a surjective connected-fiber morphism $f:(X,E)	o(Y,D)$ of smooth projective reduced simple-normal-crossing pairs, with $\mathop{\mathrm{Supp}}
olimits (f^*D)\subseteq\mathop{\mathrm{Supp}}
olimits E$, such that <i>X</i> and every boundary stratum are smooth over $Y\setminus\mathop{\mathrm{Supp}}
olimits D$. Together with logarithmic subadditivity, the inequality gives additivity, including both negative-infinity cases. This resolves Popa's logarithmic additivity conjecture positively in the projective reduced-SNC, stratum-smooth setting.

We prove the compact log-smooth Kähler case of b-semiampleness. Let $f:Y	o X$ be a surjective holomorphic map with connected fibers between smooth compact connected Kähler manifolds, and let Δ be an effective rational divisor with simple normal crossing support and coefficients in $[0,1]$, with $K_Y+\Delta\sim_{\mathbb Q}f^*L$ for $L\in\mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}
olimits (X)_{\mathbb Q}$. There is a smooth compact Kähler modification $S	o X$ for which the threshold-moduli line satisfies $M_{S_1}=
u^*M_S$ in $\mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}
olimits (S_1)_{\mathbb Q}$ for every smooth compact Kähler modification $
u:S_1	o S$, and some positive multiple of <i>M</i><sub><i>S</i></sub> is represented by a holomorphic line bundle generated by global sections. Horizontal components of coefficient one are allowed; neither projectivity nor a Campana orbifold Iitaka hypothesis is assumed.

